WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.949
♪ (intro music) ♪

00:00:05.647 --> 00:00:06.676
Give me one minute

00:00:06.676 --> 00:00:08.952
and I'll show you
how Google Cloud Operations Suite

00:00:08.952 --> 00:00:12.701
can help you monitor and troubleshoot
your infrastructure and applications.

00:00:13.177 --> 00:00:16.974
The tools in the Suite provide
deep insights and best practices

00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:21.117
that help your team's developer,
operations, DevOps, and SREs

00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:23.481
utilize Google SRE principles.

00:00:23.915 --> 00:00:25.360
It captures signals,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.008
monitors systems and services,

00:00:27.008 --> 00:00:28.990
and helps troubleshoot issues.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.750
With built-in telemetry
for Google services,

00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:33.602
out-of-box dashboards

00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:36.867
define and manage
alerts, recommendations, and more.

00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:39.658
The tools include Monitoring, Logging,

00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:41.291
and advanced observabilities,

00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:43.562
such as Trace, Debugger, and Profiler.

00:00:43.911 --> 00:00:46.653
Cloud Logging aggregates
log data at scale

00:00:46.653 --> 00:00:48.749
from all your infrastructure
and applications

00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:50.112
into a single location.

00:00:50.665 --> 00:00:52.695
This enables users to search and analyze

00:00:52.695 --> 00:00:54.155
large volumes of log

00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:56.104
to troubleshoot problems quickly,

00:00:56.104 --> 00:00:58.000
and gives you full control to decide

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.689
how and where to start the logs.

00:01:00.261 --> 00:01:02.727
Cloud Monitoring
provides visibility of health

00:01:02.727 --> 00:01:04.531
across your apps and infrastructure,

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.181
regardless of where it is.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.619
You can even define custom metrics
unique to your use case

00:01:09.619 --> 00:01:11.713
and send them to external systems.

00:01:12.308 --> 00:01:15.341
Using Metrics Explorer
and Monitoring Query Language,

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.831
you can analyze these metrics

00:01:16.831 --> 00:01:19.468
and can even set alerts and SLOs.

00:01:20.085 --> 00:01:24.253
Advanced observability feature Trace
provides insight into request flow,

00:01:24.253 --> 00:01:25.734
service topology,

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:27.555
and latency issues in your app.

00:01:27.914 --> 00:01:29.756
Debugger allows you to inspect

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.571
running applications after deployment,

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:33.830
without needing to stop or slow down.

00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:36.300
And Profiler continually analyzes

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.365
your code's performance on each service

00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:41.304
so you can improve its speed
and reduce your cost.

00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:45.296
While Trace tracks relationships
and latency between services,

00:01:45.296 --> 00:01:48.194
Profiler tracks this
across individual functions

00:01:48.194 --> 00:01:49.245
in your code base,

00:01:49.245 --> 00:01:51.374
and Debugger helps find the root cause.

00:01:51.836 --> 00:01:54.181
You can use the Cloud Console or the API

00:01:54.181 --> 00:01:56.160
to use Cloud Operation tools.

00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:59.754
All cloud capabilities offer
a very generous free tier

00:01:59.754 --> 00:02:01.708
to make it easy for you to get started.

00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:04.423
So next time you're looking
for an observability solution,

00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:06.914
take a minute to look at Cloud Operations.

00:02:06.914 --> 00:02:12.478
To learn more, check out
<i>cloud.google.com/product/operations</i>

